NOTE whisper small.en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.000
In the last video, you saw how sampling at random over the range of hyperparameters

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:09.000
can allow you to search over the space of hyperparameters more efficiently.

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:15.000
But it turns out that sampling at random doesn't mean sampling uniformly at random

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:17.000
over the range of valid values.

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:22.000
Instead, it's important to pick the appropriate scale on which to explore the hyperparameters.

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:25.000
In this video, I want to show you how to do that.

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:31.000
Let's say that you're trying to choose the number of hidden units, nL for a given layer l.

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:36.000
And let's say that you think a good range of values is somewhere from 50 to 100.

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:41.000
In that case, if you look at the number line from 50 to 100,

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:46.000
maybe picking some number of values at random within this number line

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:50.000
is a pretty reasonable way to search for this particular hyperparameter.

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:54.000
Or if you're trying to decide on the number of layers in your neural network,

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:58.000
we're calling that capital L, maybe you think the total number of layers

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:02.000
should be somewhere between 2 to 4.

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:07.000
Then sampling uniformly at random among 2, 3, and 4 might be reasonable,

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:15.000
or even using a grid search where you explicitly evaluate the values 2, 3, and 4 might be reasonable.

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:19.000
So these were a couple of examples where sampling at uniformly at random

00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:23.000
over the range of contemplating might be a reasonable thing to do.

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:26.000
But this is not true for all hyperparameters.

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:28.000
Let's look at another example.

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:33.000
Say you're searching for the hyperparameter alpha, the learning rate,

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:38.000
and let's say that you suspect 0.0001 might be on the low end,

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:41.000
or maybe it could be as high as 1.

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:49.000
Now, if you draw the number line from 0.0001 to 1,

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:55.000
and sample values at random, uniformly at random over this number line,

00:01:55.000 --> 00:02:02.000
or, you know, about 90% of the values you sample would be between 0.1 and 1.

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:06.000
So you're using 90% of the resources to search between 0.1 and 1,

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:12.000
and only 10% of the resources to search between 0.001 and 0.1.

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:14.000
So that doesn't seem right.

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:18.000
Instead, it seems more reasonable to search for hyperparameters on the log scale.

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:24.000
Where instead of using a linear scale, you'd have 0.0001 here,

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:30.000
and then 0.001, 0.01, 0.1, and then 1.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:36.000
And you instead sample uniformly at random on this type of logarithmic scale.

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:46.000
Now you have more resources dedicated to searching between 0.001 and 0.001,

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:50.000
and between 0.001 and 0.01, and so on.

00:02:50.000 --> 00:03:01.000
So in Python, the way you implement this is let r equals negative 4 times np.random.rand,

00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:08.000
and then a randomly chosen value of alpha would be alpha equals 10 to the power of r.

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:15.000
So after this first line, r will be a random number between minus 4 and 0,

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:20.000
and so alpha here will be between 10 to the minus 4 and 10 to the 0.

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:28.000
So 10 to the minus 4 is this left thing, this 10 to the minus 4, and 1 is 10 to the 0.

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:35.000
In a more general case, if you're trying to sample between 10 to the a to 10 to the b on the log scale,

00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:40.000
and in this example, this is 10 to the a,

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:45.000
and you can figure out what a is by taking the log base 10 of 0.0001,

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:51.000
which is gonna tell you a is negative 4, and this value on the right, this is 10 to the b,

00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:57.000
and you can figure out what b is by taking log base 10 of 1, which tells you b is equal to 0.

00:03:57.000 --> 00:04:04.000
So what you do is then sample r uniformly at random between a and b.

00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:07.000
So in this case, r would be between minus 4 and 0,

00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:13.000
and you can set alpha, or you randomly sample hyperparameter value, as 10 to the r.

00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:17.000
So just to recap, to sample on the log scale, you take the low value,

00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:23.000
take logs to figure out what is a, take the high value, take a log to figure out what is b.

00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:27.000
So you're now trying to sample from 10 to the a to 10 to the b on the log scale.

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:32.000
So you set r uniformly at random between a and b,

00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:35.000
and then you set the hyperparameter to be 10 to the r.

00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:39.000
So that's how you implement sampling on this logarithmic scale.

00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:45.000
Finally, one other tricky case is sampling the hyperparameter beta

00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:49.000
used for computing exponentially weighted averages.

00:04:49.000 --> 00:04:55.000
So let's say you suspect that beta should be somewhere between 0.9 to 0.999.

00:04:55.000 --> 00:04:59.000
Maybe this is the range of values you want to search over.

00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:03.000
So remember that when computing exponentially weighted averages,

00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:09.000
using 0.9 is like averaging over the last 10 values,

00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:12.000
kind of like taking the average of 10 days temperature,

00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:18.000
whereas using 0.999 is like averaging over the last 1,000 values.

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:22.000
So similar to what we saw on the last slide,

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:25.000
if you want to search between 0.9 and 0.999,

00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:28.000
it doesn't make sense to sample on a linear scale,

00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:31.000
uniformly at random between 0.9 and 0.999.

00:05:31.000 --> 00:05:35.000
So a better way to think about this is that we want to explore

00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:38.000
the range of values of 1 minus beta,

00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:43.000
which is going to now range from 0.1 to 0.001.

00:05:43.000 --> 00:05:47.000
And so we'll sample between beta,

00:05:47.000 --> 00:05:52.000
take on values from 0.1 to maybe 0.1 to 0.001.

00:05:52.000 --> 00:05:56.000
So using the method we had figured out on the previous slide,

00:05:56.000 --> 00:06:01.000
this is 10 to the minus 1, this is 10 to the minus 3.

00:06:01.000 --> 00:06:05.000
Notice on the previous slide we had the small value on the left

00:06:05.000 --> 00:06:07.000
and the large value on the right,

00:06:07.000 --> 00:06:10.000
but here we have a reverse with the large value on the left

00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:12.000
and the small value on the right.

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:16.000
So what you do is you sample r uniformly at random

00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:19.000
from negative 3 to negative 1,

00:06:19.000 --> 00:06:22.000
and you set 1 minus beta equals 10 to the r,

00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:25.000
and so beta equals 1 minus 10 to the r,

00:06:25.000 --> 00:06:29.000
and this becomes a randomly sample value of your hyperparameter

00:06:29.000 --> 00:06:31.000
chosen on the appropriate scale.

00:06:31.000 --> 00:06:34.000
And hopefully this makes sense in that this way,

00:06:34.000 --> 00:06:38.000
you spend as much resources exploring the range 0.9 to 0.99

00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:43.000
as you would exploring 0.99 to 0.999.

00:06:43.000 --> 00:06:46.000
So if you want a slightly more formal mathematical justification

00:06:46.000 --> 00:06:48.000
for why we're doing this,

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:51.000
why is it such a bad idea to sample on a linear scale?

00:06:51.000 --> 00:06:55.000
It is that when beta is close to 1,

00:06:55.000 --> 00:06:58.000
the sensitivity of the results you get changes

00:06:58.000 --> 00:07:01.000
even with very small changes to beta.

00:07:01.000 --> 00:07:10.000
So if beta goes from 0.9 to 0.9005,

00:07:10.000 --> 00:07:12.000
you know it's no big deal,

00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:15.000
this is hardly any change in your results.

00:07:15.000 --> 00:07:21.000
But if beta goes from 0.999 to 0.995,

00:07:21.000 --> 00:07:26.000
this will have a huge impact on exactly what your algorithm is doing.

00:07:26.000 --> 00:07:30.000
In both of these cases, it's averaging over roughly 10 values,

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:34.000
but here it's gone from an exponentially weighted average

00:07:34.000 --> 00:07:38.000
over about 1,000, the last 1,000 examples

00:07:38.000 --> 00:07:40.000
to now the last 2,000 examples.

00:07:40.000 --> 00:07:44.000
And it's because that formula we had, 1 over 1 minus beta,

00:07:44.000 --> 00:07:47.000
this is very sensitive to small changes in beta

00:07:47.000 --> 00:07:49.000
when beta is close to 1.

00:07:49.000 --> 00:07:52.000
So what this whole sampling process does

00:07:52.000 --> 00:07:54.000
is it causes you to sample more densely

00:07:54.000 --> 00:07:59.250
in the regime of when beta is close to 1.

00:07:59.250 --> 00:08:03.250
Or alternatively, when 1 minus beta is close to 0,

00:08:03.250 --> 00:08:07.250
so that you can be more efficient in terms of how you distribute the samples

00:08:07.250 --> 00:08:11.250
to explore the space of possible outcomes more efficiently.

00:08:11.250 --> 00:08:13.250
So I hope this helps you select the right scale

00:08:13.250 --> 00:08:16.250
on which to sample the hyperparameters.

00:08:16.250 --> 00:08:19.250
In case you don't end up making the right scaling decision

00:08:19.250 --> 00:08:21.250
on some hyperparameter choice,

00:08:21.250 --> 00:08:23.250
don't worry too much about it.

00:08:23.250 --> 00:08:25.250
Even if you sample on a uniform scale

00:08:25.250 --> 00:08:27.250
where some other scale would have been superior,

00:08:27.250 --> 00:08:29.250
you might still get okay results,

00:08:29.250 --> 00:08:31.250
especially if you use a course-defined search

00:08:31.250 --> 00:08:33.250
so that in later iterations,

00:08:33.250 --> 00:08:35.250
you focus in more on the most useful range

00:08:35.250 --> 00:08:38.250
of hyperparameter values to sample.

00:08:38.250 --> 00:08:40.250
I hope this helps you with your hyperparameter search.

00:08:40.250 --> 00:08:43.250
In the next video, I also want to share with you

00:08:43.250 --> 00:08:46.250
some thoughts on how to organize your hyperparameter search process

00:08:46.250 --> 00:08:50.250
that I hope will make your workflow a bit more efficient.
